# Action Tools

## Overview

Your support Agent needs live order data, and you already have a Jido Action
that fetches it. You'll expose that Action as a model tool, run one request
that calls it, and narrow the tool set for a single request. By the end, this
works offline:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, request)

{reply.data.status, reply.data.tool_calls, reply.data.output}
# => {:completed, 1, "Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02."}
```

| Part | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `tools` block | The Agent's static, ordered allowlist of Actions. |
| Tool definition | Name, description, and JSON Schema built from the Action. |
| Tool round | One model response with tool calls, the Action runs, then the next model call. |
| `tool_policy` | Request data that can hide tools. It can't add any. |

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

`plug` is required by `Req.Test`, which this notebook uses in place of a
provider.

## Define the Tools

A tool is a Jido Action or Flow. Its `name`, `description`, and Zoi `schema`
become the model-facing definition. Both use the same execution contract:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/lookup_order",
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up the shipping status of one order.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(description: "Order ID such as A-1001")}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, status: "shipped", eta: "2026-11-02"}}
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.IssueRefund do
  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/issue_refund",
    name: "issue_refund",
    description: "Refund part or all of one order.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), amount_cents: Zoi.integer(gte: 1)}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id, amount_cents: amount}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{refund_id: "R-#{order_id}", amount_cents: amount}}
  end
end
```

Write the `description` for the model. It's the only prose the model reads
about the tool.

## What the Model Sees

`Jido.AI.Toolset.definitions/1` builds the ReqLLM tools that go into each
model request:

```elixir
{:ok, [lookup, _refund]} = Jido.AI.Toolset.definitions([MyApp.LookupOrder, MyApp.IssueRefund])

ReqLLM.Tool.to_schema(lookup, :openai)
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  "type" => "function",
  "function" => %{
    "name" => "lookup_order",
    "description" => "Look up the shipping status of one order.",
    "strict" => true,
    "parameters" => %{
      "$schema" => "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "type" => "object",
      "properties" => %{
        "order_id" => %{"type" => "string", "description" => "Order ID such as A-1001"}
      },
      "required" => ["order_id"],
      "additionalProperties" => false,
      "propertyOrdering" => ["order_id"]
    }
  }
}
```

Every definition is `strict: true`. The Action's `id`, module, and `run/2` never
reach the provider.

## Declare the Agent

List the Actions in the `tools` block:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "support_agent",
    id: "my_app/support_agent",
    vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
    end

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
      action MyApp.IssueRefund
    end
  end
end

MyApp.SupportAgent.explain().tools
```

The result is `[MyApp.LookupOrder, MyApp.IssueRefund]`. Declaration order is
the order the model sees.

The block is the Agent's static allowlist. A bad declaration fails the build
with a `Spark.Error.DslError` ("Invalid Agent tools"). The reason comes from
`Jido.AI.Toolset.validate/1`:

| Declaration | `validate/1` reason |
| --- | --- |
| A module that isn't a Jido Action or Flow | `{:invalid_tool, module}` |
| The same module twice, or two tools with the same `name` | `:duplicate_tools` |

Any entry other than `action` or `flow` inside `tools` fails with
`tools accepts only action or flow declarations`.

## Offline Model

These helpers stand in for the OpenAI Responses API. The first returns one
`function_call`; the second returns a text answer. Each stub also sends the
decoded request body to this notebook so you can inspect it:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.FakeOpenAI do
  def function_call(call_id, name, arguments) do
    response("resp-tool", [
      %{
        "type" => "function_call",
        "id" => "fc-#{call_id}",
        "call_id" => call_id,
        "name" => name,
        "arguments" => Jason.encode!(arguments),
        "status" => "completed"
      }
    ])
  end

  def text(text) do
    response("resp-text", [
      %{
        "type" => "message",
        "id" => "msg-text",
        "role" => "assistant",
        "status" => "completed",
        "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
      }
    ])
  end

  def reply(owner, body) do
    fn conn ->
      {:ok, raw, conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
      send(owner, {:model_request, Jason.decode!(raw)})
      Req.Test.json(conn, body)
    end
  end

  defp response(id, output) do
    %{
      "id" => id,
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "error" => nil,
      "incomplete_details" => nil,
      "parallel_tool_calls" => true,
      "output" => output,
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 20,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 5,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 25
      }
    }
  end
end

offline = %{
  api_key: "offline-test-key",
  max_retries: 0,
  req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]
}
```

Pass `offline` as the request `options`. To call a real provider, drop
`req_http_options` and `api_key` and set `OPENAI_API_KEY`.

## Run Two Rounds

Queue the two model responses, in order:

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(
  MyApp.LLM,
  MyApp.FakeOpenAI.reply(self(), MyApp.FakeOpenAI.function_call("call-1", "lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"}))
)

Req.Test.expect(
  MyApp.LLM,
  MyApp.FakeOpenAI.reply(
    self(),
    MyApp.FakeOpenAI.text("Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02.")
  )
)
```

Run one request as data with `cmd/2`:

```elixir
agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-1")

request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    options: offline
  })

{:ok, agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, request)

{reply.data.status, reply.data.model_calls, reply.data.tool_rounds, reply.data.tool_calls,
 reply.data.output}
```

The result is
`{:completed, 2, 1, 1, "Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02."}`.

ReAct made one model call, validated the `lookup_order` call against the
Action schema, ran `MyApp.LookupOrder`, recorded the result, and made a second
model call.

## Inspect the Requests

Read the two request bodies that the stubs captured:

```elixir
requests =
  for _ <- 1..2 do
    receive do
      {:model_request, body} -> body
    after
      1_000 -> :missing
    end
  end

[first, second] = requests

{Enum.map(first["tools"], & &1["name"]),
 Enum.map(second["input"], &(&1["type"] || &1["role"]))}
```

The result is
`{["lookup_order", "issue_refund"], ["system", "user", "function_call", "function_call_output"]}`.

The second request carries the model's `function_call` and the correlated
`function_call_output`:

```elixir
second["input"] |> List.last() |> Map.take(["call_id", "output"])
```

The result is
`%{"call_id" => "call-1", "output" => ~s({"eta":"2026-11-02","order_id":"A-1001","status":"shipped"})}`.

## Read the Tool Attempt

The reply lists each executed call:

```elixir
[attempt] = reply.data.tool_attempts

Map.take(attempt, [:tool_call_id, :tool_name, :status, :input, :output, :tool_round])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  tool_call_id: "call-1",
  tool_name: "lookup_order",
  status: :ok,
  input: %{"order_id" => "A-1001"},
  output: %{"eta" => "2026-11-02", "order_id" => "A-1001", "status" => "shipped"},
  tool_round: 1
}
```

The same record is in the committed Session as an `:ai_tool_attempt` entry.
[Tool Execution and Results](tool-execution-and-results.md) covers its
fields, error results, and size limits.

## Narrow One Request

`tool_policy` is part of the ReAct request schema. It selects a subset of the
allowlist for one request:

| Field | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `visible` | `[]` | Keep only these names. `[]` keeps all. |
| `hidden` | `[]` | Remove these names after `visible`. |
| `review` | `[]` | Require approval before these run. |
| `requirement` | `:optional` | `:optional`, `:required`, `:per_turn`, or `:disabled`. |
| `reason` | `nil` | Text recorded with the selection and shown in review requests. |

Hide the refund tool from a status question:

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(
  MyApp.LLM,
  MyApp.FakeOpenAI.reply(self(), MyApp.FakeOpenAI.text("I can only look up orders here."))
)

request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Refund order A-1001",
    options: offline,
    tool_policy: %{hidden: ["issue_refund"], reason: "status questions only"}
  })

{:ok, agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, request)

receive do
  {:model_request, body} -> {reply.data.status, Enum.map(body["tools"], & &1["name"])}
end
```

The result is `{:completed, ["lookup_order"]}`. A hidden tool isn't sent, and
a call to it fails validation before any Action runs.

`requirement` maps to the provider's tool choice:

| `requirement` | First model call | After a tool round | Final answer allowed |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `:optional` | no `tool_choice` | no `tool_choice` | always |
| `:required` | `:required` | `:auto` | after one tool round |
| `:per_turn` | `:required` | `:required` | never while tools are visible |
| `:disabled` | `:none`, no tools sent | — | always |

A `tool_choice` in `options` is ignored. Set the requirement instead.

## Requests Can't Add Tools

Every name in `tool_policy` must already be in the allowlist. An unknown name
fails the request before the model runs:

```elixir
request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Close my account",
    options: offline,
    tool_policy: %{visible: ["delete_account"]}
  })

{:error, error} = MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, request)
{error.__struct__, error.details.reason}
```

The result is
`{Jido.Action.Error.ExecutionFailureError, {:unknown_tool, "delete_account"}}`.
The `start_request` step failed, so no model call was made. Jido also logs a
warning for the failed step.

Other selection errors:

| Policy | Error reason |
| --- | --- |
| `requirement: :required` with every tool hidden | `{:tool_requirement_without_visible_tools, :required}` |
| `review: ["issue_refund"]` with `issue_refund` hidden | `{:review_tool_not_visible, "issue_refund"}` |
| The same name twice in one list | `:duplicate_tool_policy_names` |

A trusted execution host can add tools at run time.
[Dynamic Tool Sources](dynamic-tool-sources.md) covers that path. Request data
never can.

## Summary

- A model tool is a Jido Action or Flow listed in the Agent's `tools` block.
- The model sees the target's `name`, `description`, and a strict JSON Schema
  built from its Zoi `schema`.
- The `tools` block accepts Actions and Flows, with unique modules and names.
- One tool round is a model call with tool calls, the Actions, then another
  model call that carries the correlated results.
- `tool_policy` can hide, require, or review tools for one request. Any name it
  uses must already be allowed.

